  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThis Afternoon-Tonight...\n\nTemperatures this afternoon are currently sitting in the mid 80s-low \n90s, under mostly sunny skies. Winds are generally light with \ngusts around 15mph or less. Aloft, the area is under \nnorthwesterly flow with ridging over the western U.S. and \ntroughing over the northeastern U.S. Lows tonight will be in the\n50s to low 60s. \n\nFriday...\n\nAnother well above normal day is expected across the area on Friday, \nwith highs climbing into the mid-upper 80s (normal highs upper 60s-\nlow 70s). Southwesterly winds will be breezy throughout the late \nmorning-early evening, gusting 20-30 mph. Outside of a few passing \ncirrus clouds, sunny skies are expected, resulting in a pleasant \n(but breezy) end to the school week. \n\nThis weekend...\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft transitions to southwesterly flow this \nweekend as troughing develops over the west coast. Seasonably warm \nweather continues on Saturday with highs in the mid-upper 80s. A \ntightening pressure gradient over the plains results in a windy day \non Saturday. Southerly winds of 15-25 mph with gusts of 30-40 mph \nare expected during the daytime hours though an isolate/occasional \ngust over 40 mph can't be ruled out. Warm and breezy weather \ncontinues on Sunday with highs once again climbing into the \n80s. A passing disturbance pushes a cold front into the area \nSunday evening/night though precipitation is not expected with \nthis frontal passage. \n\nNext Week...\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft remains in place through the end of the \nforecast period. Under this flow rainfall chances to the area as \ndisturbances move through the area. The most substantial chance \nlooks to be Tuesday night-Wednesday as a more robust trough lifts \nacross the Plains. There remains plenty of spread in model guidance \non the exact track of the low/trough and resulting impacts. 12z\nensemble/deterministic guidance hints at the potential for the \narea to be in the \"dry slot\" of the low, with rain falling \nmainly north/east of the forecast area. This system will \ncontinue to be monitored closely over the coming days. Highs \nwill generally be seasonable-seasonably warm, though the exact \ntrack of the aforementioned system will impact how warm/cool \neach day gets.",
